You can use C#'s code parsing library (typically Roslyn, but there are others) to perform this task. Here's a template you can use to implement this, parsing the syntax tree and looking for nodes with comments starting with "@@@":

```cs
class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        // Create a C# code string
        string code = @"
... your code here ...";
        var testMethod = CSharpSyntaxTree.ParseText(code).GetRoot()
            .DescendantNodes()
            .OfType<MethodDeclarationSyntax>()
            .First(m => m.Identifier.Text == "YourMethodName");
            
       // Go through each line in the method declaration
       foreach (var line in testMethod.GetText().ToString().Split('\n'))
       {
            // Check if the line of code has a comment with "@@@"
            if(line.Trim().StartsWith("// @@@"))
            {
                // Extract the part after "// @@@" to create a debug assertion
                var assertionLine = line.Replace("// @@@", "Debug.Assert(");
                // If the assertion needs a semicolon at the end, remove it
                assertionLine = assertionLine.Trim().TrimEnd(';');
                // Add a semicolon at the end
                assertionLine += ");";
                
                System.Console.WriteLine(assertionLine);
            }
        }
    }
}
```
In the code above, replace "YourMethodName" with the actual name of your method.

This is a simple solution and may not handle the more complex C# syntax properly. For example, it does not consider scenarios where a line of code contains commas at the end of multiple statements, conditional statements, etc. For a complete solution, consider using Roslyn or a similar C# parsing library.--------------------